in @prism

in @optics
in @glass
in @nl
in @mirror/lens
in @mirror/lens/cli

# @mirror/lens/cli/shatter — materialize AST -> text as a stage.
#
# Third of eight verb sub-stages forward-promised in
# docs/specs/cli-as-prism.md §3 (table at §3 + §3.1 shape sketch).
# Realizes the `command shatter` declaration in mirror.spec at the
# stage altitude — the substrate level where the AST-projection
# pipeline (focus -> project -> shift -> settle) writes the canonical
# `.shatter` artifact.
#
# === Why shatter is a stage, not a top-level shift ===
#
# Per cli-as-prism §2.1: shatter could have been `mirror shift --emit
# shatter` at the top level, but shatter has its own lifecycle — the
# `.shatter` file is a first-class artifact per cybernetic-cli §3.
# Sub-stage earns it: the full algebra (focus what would shatter,
# project to a single target altitude, split enumerate variants,
# shift re-emit at a different altitude, settle write the file) is
# present on the shatter sub-manifold.
#
# Per §1.8: the TERMINAL `.shatter` WRITE is `detector`-shaped at the
# sink — the final settle terminates the chain at a content-addressed
# write boundary, the detector that measures the projection and
# records the verdict. The CLI sub-stage that drives the projection
# is the `stage` upstream of that detector. The stage produces the
# beam; the detector collapses it to the canonical text form.
#
# === Family placement ===
#
# Parent bench: @mirror/lens/cli (shards/mirror/lens/cli.mirror).
# Family root:  @mirror/lens    (shards/mirror/lens.mirror).
# Sibling stages:
#   @mirror/lens/cli/compile    — landed at 737330f (the build pipeline)
#   @mirror/lens/cli/kintsugi   — landed previously this tick
#   @mirror/lens/cli/bootstrap  — autopoietic phase machine (next)
#   @mirror/lens/cli/join       — peer-entry (Glanville's verb)
#   @mirror/lens/cli/reflect    — VSM System 5 view
#   @mirror/lens/cli/time       — Elm-style time travel
#   @mirror/lens/cli/crack      — third-state lifecycle
#
# Path-namespace property: this file at
# `shards/mirror/lens/cli/shatter.mirror` declares
# `@mirror/lens/cli/shatter` and only that.
#
# Spec context: docs/specs/cli-as-prism.md §3, §3.1 (shape sketch),
#               §2.1 (shatter sub-stage decision; detector framing),
#               §7 (default-op rule: shatter declares `default settle`
#               — the only other stage besides compile to do so;
#               both are "produce an output artifact" acts),
#               docs/specs/optical-keywords.md §1.2 (stage),
#               §1.8 (detector — the terminal sink shape),
#               mirror.spec (`command shatter { arg oid:
#               content_address; arg out: ~f }`).
# Substrate decisions: [[architecture-splinter-ast-quote-primitive]]
#                      (splinter(ast) is the substrate's parametric
#                      quote mechanism; .shatter is the serialized
#                      form of that quote at the @mirror/store sink),
#                      [[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]]
#                      (stage IS a sub-prism specialization),
#                      [[architecture-shards-as-substrate-source]]
#                      (file path matches namespace),
#                      [[architecture-lift-as-load-bearing]] (shift =
#                      re-emit at a different altitude; same content,
#                      different basis).
# Related shards: lens/cli.mirror (parent bench),
#                 lens/cli/compile.mirror (sibling — the other
#                 `default settle` stage; symmetric artifact-write
#                 shape),
#                 lens/cli/kintsugi.mirror (sibling — kintsugi's
#                 emit_shatter flag composes with this stage's
#                 settle at the @mirror/store seam),
#                 glass.mirror (imperfect / transparency carriers),
#                 mirror/store.mirror (the @mirror/store/oid type the
#                 oid arg references; the content-addressed sink the
#                 detector writes into).

stage @mirror/lens/cli/shatter {
  # Per cli-as-prism §7: shatter declares `default settle` — the
  # canonical safe-write default. Bare `mirror shatter <oid>`
  # materializes the .shatter projection; the stage name plus a
  # content-address is enough because the act is a side-effect-
  # bounded artifact write (one file, one proof, no escalation).
  # The other stage with `default settle` is compile (the build);
  # all other sibling stages (kintsugi, bootstrap, join, reflect,
  # time, crack) inherit `default focus`.
  default settle

  # The five operations the produced prism exposes on the shatter
  # sub-manifold (a settled subgraph being projected to .shatter
  # text). Each operand below names the natural shape of the
  # manifold's element at that op's slot, per the spec §3.1 sketch
  # convention and §2.1's per-verb decisions:
  #   focus    target     — show what would shatter (no write)
  #   project  predicate  — filter to one target altitude
  #                          (project = orthogonal projection;
  #                          [[architecture-operations-as-linear-algebra]])
  #   split    variant    — enumerate materialization variants
  #                          (alt formats / pretty-printer choices)
  #   shift    altitude   — re-emit at a different declared altitude
  #                          (same content, different basis;
  #                          [[architecture-lift-as-load-bearing]])
  #   settle   target     — the ONE write; write the `.shatter` file
  #                          at the @mirror/store sink (detector-
  #                          shaped per §1.8); emits proof block
  focus    target
  project  predicate
  split    variant
  shift    altitude
  settle   target
}

# === Shatter-specific actions ===
#
# Realizes mirror.spec's `command shatter { arg oid: content_address;
# arg out: ~f }` at the stage altitude. The action signature carries
# the same shape — a content-addressed handle to the settled shard
# plus the file destination — at the substrate altitude so the
# dispatcher can walk from cli surface to settled call without leaving
# the substrate. The Rust runtime walks these declarations; it does
# not name commands. Substrate-pull discipline per
# [[architecture-fragmentation-is-the-rust-substrate]].
#
# Honest `partials` from birth (per recognition #306): the return type
# is the substrate's `imperfect` functor (declared in
# shards/glass.mirror as `imperfect(a, e, l) = success(a) |
# partial(a, l) | failure(e, l)`). A shatter projection that closes
# every fragment cleanly returns success; a projection that lifts loss
# (e.g. an opacity-tagged Dark region that cannot be rendered
# unambiguously) returns partial-with-loss carrying the
# transparency-tagged residual opacity, so the artifact's reader sees
# the unresolved fracture rather than a silently-incomplete file. Per
# [[feedback-manual-closure-is-training-pull]]: Dark regions are
# kintsugi's mutation targets, NOT manual-patch targets at the shatter
# sink; the partial-with-loss shape preserves them for the kintsugi
# loop upstream.

# shatter(oid, out) — project the settled shard at content-address
# `oid` to the canonical `.shatter` text form, write it to `out`.
# Returns the imperfect carrying the written file's ref on success,
# the partial-with-loss on tolerable opacity (the renderer's
# best-effort projection with the residual opacity map attached),
# the failure with reason on hard error (oid not in store, write
# permission denied, etc.).
shatter(oid: ref, out: ref) -> imperfect { \ }

out @mirror/lens/cli/shatter
out shatter
